What is the Username-Password Flow?

This is the simplest but least secure OAuth flow. Here's how it works:

Your app sends your actual Salesforce username and password directly in one HTTP request to Salesforce, along with the app's client ID/secret, and Salesforce sends back a token immediately. No login screen, no redirect, no approval popup — just: "here's my username and password, give me a token."

Think back to the "hotel key card" analogy from before — this flow is like telling the front desk your password over the phone instead of showing ID at the counter. It skips the safer, more visual process entirely.

Why is this risky?
The password is transmitted directly in the request — if intercepted, exposed in logs, or leaked, the attacker has your actual login credentials, not just a limited token.
There's no place for multi-factor authentication (MFA) to happen — the flow is a single API call, no room for that extra "prove it's really you" step.
It's an old-school pattern that predates more secure alternatives designed specifically to avoid ever handling raw passwords.

That's exactly why Salesforce is retiring it.

What's changing, and when?
Date	What happens
Summer '26 and later	Brand new orgs can't even turn this on — the setting is disabled/grayed out from day one
February 20, 2027	Enforcement — the flow stops working entirely for existing orgs/integrations that still use it

(Note: this date got pushed back once already — originally planned for Winter '27, now moved to Feb 20, 2027 — so Salesforce is giving extra runway here.)

What should you switch to instead?

Salesforce recommends two replacements, based on what kind of integration you have:

A real person logging in through your app?
→ Use the Web-Server Flow with PKCE. This is the "redirect to Salesforce's login page, user approves access" flow we discussed earlier — the normal, secure OAuth pattern.
Server-to-server integration with no human involved (e.g., a nightly batch job, a backend service syncing data)?
→ Use the Client Credentials Flow. This is designed exactly for this case — the app authenticates as itself (not as a specific user) using a client ID/secret, no password ever involved.
How to check if THIS affects your application

Same approach as before — check both config and code:

Config side:

Setup → Quick Find → Release Updates → look for "Retirement of OAuth 2.0 Username-Password Flow for Connected Apps"
Setup → Quick Find → OAuth and OpenID Connect Settings → check if "Allow OAuth Username-Password Flows" is enabled

Code side:
Look at your app's authentication code for a token request with:

grant_type=password

If you see that anywhere in your integration code — that confirms you're using this flow, and it will break on Feb 20, 2027 unless migrated.

One thing worth noting: this is a much higher-impact update than the device flow one — device flow is a niche use case (mostly TVs/IoT), but username-password flow is a very common (if outdated) integration pattern, especially in older or simpler backend scripts. So this one's worth checking more urgently.

Want to go through your Connected Apps list together (like we started with the App Manager screen) to check for this specific flow?

Setup → Quick Find → OAuth and OpenID Connect Settings → check if "Allow OAuth Username-Password Flows" is enabled
yes this is enabled in our org

Okay, that's useful information — here's what it means for you.

What "enabled" tells you (and what it doesn't)

This setting being on means: username-password flow is currently allowed to work in your org, if any app tries to use it.

It does not necessarily mean your application is actually using it right now — it's more like a door being unlocked. The door being unlocked doesn't mean someone's walking through it. So this is a good first signal, but not final proof.

Next step: find out if anything is actually walking through that door

You need to check whether any of your Connected Apps actually authenticate using this flow. A few ways to narrow it down:

1. Check your integration/application code directly
Look for any HTTP request to Salesforce's token endpoint that includes:

grant_type=password

This is usually in your backend integration code, middleware, or any script that logs into Salesforce programmatically (not through a browser login).

2. Check Login History in Salesforce

Setup → Quick Find → Login History
Look at the Authentication Method Reference or Application columns
Entries showing login via a Connected App without a browser-based redirect are often username-password flow logins

3. Ask around / check documentation
If your application was built a while ago, or by a previous developer/team, there might be internal documentation or a config file (like environment variables such as SF_USERNAME, SF_PASSWORD alongside a client ID/secret) — that combination is a strong sign of username-password flow.

Why this setting might be "on" even if unused

Many orgs just have this enabled by default from years ago (older orgs especially, since new orgs from Summer '26 onward can't even enable it). So it's very possible this was turned on long ago for some integration that may or may not still be relevant — worth confirming with your team if others maintain integrations you don't know about.
